<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duke Xiang of Qin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 766 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Duke Xiang of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> ruled the Qin people from 777 to 766 BCE. In 770 BCE, after the sack of the western Zhou capital, he escorted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-ping-of-zhou">King Ping of Zhou</a> eastward to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengzhou">Luoyi</a>. In return the king raised him to the rank of a feudal lord and promised Qin the old Zhou lands in the west if it could take them from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rong-people">Rong</a>, the formal beginning of Qin as a state.</p></div>
